  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Why the letters are round",
      "body": "<p>Odia was written for centuries by scratching palm leaves with an iron stylus, then rubbing soot into the scratches. A long straight line along the top, like the Devanagari headline, would follow the grain of the leaf and split it, so scribes drew it as a curve instead. That is the round cap that sits on almost every letter: compare Odia <span lang=\"or\">କ ଗ ମ</span> with Hindi <span lang=\"hi\">क ग म</span>.</p>\n<p>Because the caps all look alike, the part to watch is the body under the cap.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Odia keeps its o",
      "body": "<p>Every consonant carries a short vowel, said o as in hot. Hindi, Bengali and Gujarati drop that vowel at the end of most words, but Odia keeps it: <span lang=\"or\">ଘର</span> (house) is ghara, not ghar, and <span lang=\"or\">କମଳ</span> (lotus) is kamaḷa.</p>\n<p>To end a word on a bare consonant, Odia writes the halanta, <span lang=\"or\">କ୍</span>, or joins the consonant to the next one.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Letters that sound the same",
      "body": "<p>Spelling keeps the letters of the Sanskrit alphabet, but speech has merged some of them:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ଶ ଷ ସ</td><td>all said s</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ଜ ଯ</td><td>both said j; the y sound is written <span lang=\"or\">ୟ</span>, with a dot</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ଇ ଈ, ଉ ଊ</td><td>short and long vowels said alike</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ଣ ନ</td><td>often said alike</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>So learn which letter a word uses by sight, as English speakers learn ph and f.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Conjuncts",
      "body": "<p>When two consonants meet with no vowel between them, the second is usually written smaller, under or beside the first:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ନ୍ + ତ = ନ୍ତ</td><td>nt, as in <span lang=\"or\">ଦାନ୍ତ</span> (tooth)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ମ୍ + ବ = ମ୍ବ</td><td>mb, as in <span lang=\"or\">ଆମ୍ବ</span> (mango)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"or\">ଶ୍ + ୱ = ଶ୍ୱ</td><td>św, as in <span lang=\"or\">ଈଶ୍ୱର</span> (God)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>A <span lang=\"or\">ର</span> before a consonant becomes a small hook on top, as in <span lang=\"or\">ଊର୍ମି</span>.</p>"
    }
  ]
